    {
      "id": "sol.amc-12a-2015-p14.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "By the change-of-base formula, \\(\\dfrac{1}{\\log_b a} = \\log_a b.\\) Therefore \\[ \\begin{aligned} &1 = \\log_a 2 + \\log_a 3 \\\\ &\\quad {}+ \\log_a 4 = \\log_a 24. \\end{aligned} \\]\n\nIt follows that \\(a = 24.\\)\n\nThus, the correct answer is D.",
      "hints": [
        "\\(\\dfrac{1}{\\log_b a} = \\log_a b\\)",
        "The sum becomes \\(\\log_a(2\\cdot 3\\cdot 4)\\)"
      ],
